{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790888570,"nonce":"rgJfOdN9V58Q31pRK7yMBQ5RI7M=","title":"No incident-tool provider exists, so an Incident cannot become one external incident that later facts update","message":"**What is wrong.** ADR 0052 §5: the outbound mapping binds an Incident to `(destination, external incident id)` the way a ChannelThread binds a Conversation to a Slack root, and oto sends facts, never commands — never PagerDuty's `resolve`. The generic webhook returns no external id, so nothing can be updated in place.\n\n**The demonstration.** `notification_deliveries.provider_message_id` (`db/migrations/00011_notification.sql:271`) is documented as \"Slack ts of THIS message\" only.\n\n**Why it matters.** Without the mapping every later fact becomes a new external incident.\n\n**Where.** A new Channel provider; the mapping row; provider docs. **Human decision needed** — which tool first, with a sandbox account to verify against, weighing two facts about PagerDuty Events v2 that bear on the ADR: it **requires a `severity`** on every trigger, which oto must take from the alert's own label (never invent one — a human-set or oto-set severity is banned), with a stated rule for Cases that carry none and for an Incident whose members disagree; and it **cannot update an incident in place** — later facts can only be re-triggers on the same `dedup_key` or notes via the REST API, so \"facts as updates on the same external incident\" needs the REST API or a weaker reading. incident.io's API has neither constraint.\n\n**Scope.** medium.\n\n**Done when.** The owner has picked the first provider with the two PagerDuty constraints answered; drawing an Incident creates one external incident; later facts land on it; the provider has no code path that sends resolve, close or a status change (a test asserts no request body carries one); provider docs say auto-resolve is the operator's own rule to write.\n\n**Blocked by.** `aa6d18b`\n\n**Mode.** HITL — needs a human decision or review before it can be implemented. Source: ADR 0052/0053/0054 design session, 2026-10-02.","files":null}]}